
File: wave.info, Node: Top, Up: (dir)


LinuxWave installs each package into its own versioned directory under
the install root, keeps every version side by side, and maintains
unversioned links so a package can be called with or without a version.
Packages are fetched from a mirror or from LinuxWave's own bottle store;
nothing is compiled on the user's machine.

* Menu:

* install::	Install a package, or one specific version of it. Several
        packages can be given at once, separated by commas or
        spaces; each one is installed on its own, so a failure does
        not stop the rest.
* upgrade::	Upgrade a package to its latest version; with --all, every
        installed package. Old versions are kept — -X,
        --remove-old-versions removes them in the same run.
* reinstall::	Download and install the same version again, replacing what
        is on disk. Useful when a package was rebuilt under the same
        version number, when files were damaged (it is the fix `wave
        doctor` suggests), or to pin a package back to the version
        already installed instead of upgrading.
* uninstall::	Uninstall one package version, several of them, or every
        version. Packages can be listed with commas or spaces, and
        one package can name several versions (ffmpeg@6.1@6.0). The
        unversioned link falls back to the highest remaining
        version, and goes away with the last one.
* transfer::	Rewrite the RPATH of already installed ELF files so they no
        longer name an absolute install path, then leave them ready
        to run from anywhere. Packages that are already relative are
        detected and skipped, so the command is safe to repeat.
        Dependencies used by the named packages are transferred too,
        otherwise the tree still could not be moved.
* list::	List the packages you have installed, with their versions,
        in two columns.
* search::	Search the online package index.
* info::	Show author, description, homepage and the installed /
        available versions.
* selfupdate::	Update LinuxWave itself to the version published in the
        self-update repository.
* link::	Create a versionless shortcut, or re-point it at another
        installed version. Installs and uninstalls keep it up to
        date afterwards.
* unlink::	Remove the versionless shortcut. A later uninstall will not
        re-create it.
* linkquery::	Show which version the versionless shortcut points at.
* mirror::	Show the address each kind of data is fetched from,
        including the built-in defaults for anything not overridden
        by an environment variable.
* latest::	Compare what you have with the newest version in the index;
        nothing is downloaded.
* basedir::	Print LinuxWave's install root -- the base_dir in
        config.json, which is where the binaries, the links and the
        dependencies live. A path under your home directory is shown
        with ~. With --raw it prints the absolute path as-is and
        nothing else.
* doctor::	Look for things that are already broken but rarely noticed:
        a version directory without its executable, a dangling link,
        a package that was never linked, an orphaned dependency
        tree, an ELF file whose RPATH still names an absolute path.
        Nothing is changed; every finding comes with the command
        that fixes it. The exit status is the number of problems
        found.
* shellenv::	Print the shell code that prepends LinuxWave's directories
        to PATH. You eval it once from your shell profile; it does
        nothing on its own.
* version::	Print the installed LinuxWave version.
* manual::	Show the full manual — the same text that ships as man pages
        and info files — or just one command's section.

* GLOBAL FLAGS::

  the complete manual。--zh to read the Chinese edition


File: wave.info, Node: GLOBAL FLAGS, Up: Top


  -s, --silent
      Print nothing at all; the exit code is the signal (beats -v;
      install / upgrade / uninstall / link / unlink / selfupdate)

  -S, --show-error
      With -s, still print the error when a command fails (without the
      🌊, so scripts can parse it)

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  --ver <string>
      Install a specific version (install only; --ver=1.0 works too)

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package
      declares

  --install-python-from-source
      Fetch the bundled Python from its upstream URL instead of
      LinuxWave's own repository

  LINUXWAVE_PIP_INDEX
      Env var. pip index for the Python deps; default pypi.org/simple

  LINUXWAVE_CACERT
      Env var. PEM file with extra root certificates for corporate TLS
      inspection or a private-CA mirror (unset = system trust store)



File: wave.info, Node: install, Next: upgrade, Up: Top


Install a package, or one specific version of it. Several packages can
be given at once, separated by commas or spaces; each one is installed
on its own, so a failure does not stop the rest.

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  --no-link
      Do not touch the unversioned link (the old name --unlink still
      works)

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package
      declares

  --raw
      Plain output for logs and scripts: no emoji, no colours, ASCII
      progress bar

  -v, --verbose
      Verbose mode

Examples:

     wave install jq
     wave install ffmpeg@6.1
     wave install jq, yq
     wave install jq yq fx@40.0.2
     wave install jq --limit-rate 1M
     wave install jq --max-time=1min


