Upgrade a package to its latest version; with --all, every installed
package. Old versions are kept — -X, --remove-old-versions removes them
in the same run.

  -a, --all
      Upgrade every installed package that has a newer version

  -X, --remove-old-versions
      Remove the older versions as part of the upgrade

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running macOS is older than the package
      declares

  --raw
      Plain output for logs and scripts: no emoji, no colours, ASCII
      progress bar

  -v, --verbose
      Verbose mode

Examples:

     wave upgrade jq
     wave upgrade jq -X
     wave upgrade --all
     wave upgrade jq --remove-old-versions --max-time=2min


File: wave.info, Node: reinstall, Next: uninstall, Prev: upgrade, Up: Top


Download and install the same version again, replacing what is on disk.
Useful when a package was rebuilt under the same version number, when
files were damaged (it is the fix `wave doctor` suggests), or to pin a
package back to the version already installed instead of upgrading.

  -a, --all
      Reinstall every installed package

  -C, --continue
      Resume an interrupted download

  --proxy <string>
      HTTP/HTTPS proxy (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:8080)

  --skip-ssl
      Skip SSL certificate verification (insecure)

  --limit-rate <string>
      Limit download speed (e.g. 200K, 1M, 5M; a bare number means
      bytes)

  --max-time <string>
      Maximum total time for one request (40s, 1min, 1min20s, 2h; a bare
      number means seconds)

  --connect-timeout <string>
      Maximum time for the handshake

  --content-time <string>
      Maximum time to receive data

  --no-link
      Do not touch the unversioned link (the old name --unlink still
      works)

  -F, --force
      Install anyway when the running system is older than the package
      declares

  --raw
      Plain output for logs and scripts: no emoji, no colours, ASCII
      progress bar

Examples:

     wave reinstall jq
     wave reinstall jq@1.8.2
     wave reinstall jq, yq
     wave reinstall --all


File: wave.info, Node: uninstall, Next: transfer, Prev: reinstall, Up: Top


Uninstall one package version, several of them, or every version.
Packages can be listed with commas or spaces, and one package can name
several versions (ffmpeg@6.1@6.0). The unversioned link falls back to
the highest remaining version, and goes away with the last one.

  --no-link
      Do not touch the unversioned link (the old name --unlink still
      works)

  -a, --all
      Uninstall every installed version of the package (the same as
      @all)

Examples:

     wave uninstall jq
     wave uninstall ffmpeg@6.1
     wave uninstall ffmpeg@6.1@6.0
     wave uninstall jq, yq
     wave uninstall ffmpeg -a


File: wave.info, Node: transfer, Next: list, Prev: uninstall, Up: Top


Rewrite the RPATH of already installed ELF files so they no longer name
an absolute install path, then leave them ready to run from anywhere.
Packages that are already relative are detected and skipped, so the
command is safe to repeat. Dependencies used by the named packages are
transferred too, otherwise the tree still could not be moved.

  -a, --all
      Transfer every installed package and dependency

  -n, --dry-run
      Only report which packages would be transferred

Examples:

     wave transfer jq
     wave transfer jq, yq
     wave transfer --all --dry-run


