File: wave.info, Node: basedir, Next: doctor, Prev: latest, Up: Top


Print LinuxWave's install root -- the base_dir in config.json, which is
where the binaries, the links and the dependencies live. A path under
your home directory is shown with ~. With --raw it prints the absolute
path as-is and nothing else.

  --raw
      Print the absolute path as-is, with no emoji or colour (for
      scripts)

  --set <path>
      Move the whole install tree to <path> and update base_dir

  -n, --dry-run
      With --set: report what would be copied and which config file
      would change, then stop

Examples:

     wave basedir
     wave basedir --raw
     cd "$(wave basedir --raw)"


File: wave.info, Node: doctor, Next: shellenv, Prev: basedir, Up: Top


Look for things that are already broken but rarely noticed: a version
directory without its executable, a dangling link, a package that was
never linked, an orphaned dependency tree, an ELF file whose RPATH still
names an absolute path. Nothing is changed; every finding comes with the
command that fixes it. The exit status is the number of problems found.

  --raw
      Machine-readable: one 'severity<TAB>check<TAB>detail' line per
      finding

  --list-checks
      List the check names and what each one looks at

Examples:

     wave doctor
     wave doctor links
     wave doctor --raw
     wave doctor --list-checks


